+To evaluate how well a model generalizes to new data, the data is split into a
+training set and a test set, commonly in an 80/20 ratio. Other proportions can
+be used depending on the size of the dataset and the application.
+
+Training is usually combined with cross-validation to make the model more
+robust. In K-fold cross-validation, the data is divided into K folds (subsets)
+of similar size. The model is trained on K-1 folds and validated on the
+remaining fold. This is repeated K times so that each fold serves as the
+validation set once (@fig-cv).
+
+```{r}
+#| label: fig-cv
+#| fig-cap: "5-fold cross-validation. The data is first split into a training set (80%) and a test set (20%). The training set is divided into five folds. In each iteration, the model is trained on four folds and validated on the remaining fold. The test set is used only for the final evaluation."
+#| fig-width: 8
+#| fig-height: 3.5
+#| echo: false
+
+library(ggplot2)
+
+k <- 5
+# One tile per fold and iteration. The fold that matches the iteration is used
+# for validation.
+cv_df <- expand.grid(fold = seq_len(k), iteration = seq_len(k))
+cv_df$role <- ifelse(cv_df$fold == cv_df$iteration, "Validation", "Training")
+cv_df$x <- cv_df$fold
+# The test set is held out in every iteration
+test_df <- data.frame(
+    x = k + 1.5, iteration = seq_len(k), role = "Test", fold = NA
+)
+cv_df <- rbind(cv_df, test_df)
+cv_df$role <- factor(cv_df$role, levels = c("Training", "Validation", "Test"))
+
+ggplot(cv_df, aes(x = x, y = iteration, fill = role)) +
+    geom_tile(
+        aes(width = ifelse(role == "Test", 1.25, 0.95)),
+        height = 0.8, colour = "white"
+    ) +
+    geom_text(
+        data = cv_df[cv_df$role != "Test", ],
+        aes(label = paste("Fold", fold)),
+        size = 3.2
+    ) +
+    annotate(
+        "text",
+        x = c((k + 1) / 2, k + 1.5), y = 0.25,
+        label = c("Training set (80%)", "Test set (20%)"),
+        fontface = "bold", size = 3.8
+    ) +
+    scale_y_reverse(
+        breaks = seq_len(k), labels = paste("Iteration", seq_len(k))
+    ) +
+    scale_fill_manual(
+        values = c(
+            Training = "#A9CCEB", Validation = "#F6B26B",
+            Test = "grey70"
+        ),
+        name = NULL
+    ) +
+    theme_minimal() +
+    theme(
+        panel.grid = element_blank(),
+        axis.title = element_blank(),
+        axis.text.x = element_blank(),
+        legend.position = "bottom"
+    )
+```
+
+When the dataset is small, splitting it into training and test sets might not
+be feasible. In such cases, cross-validation alone can provide a rough estimate
+of the model's performance. While this approach is not as reliable as having a
+separate test set, it still indicates how well the model might perform on new
+data.
+
